From 2166b2ab938750f3ba976c5c560e0f6256b795ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yasuhiro Niji <aiseed-dev@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 14:45:25 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] [se] Show numbers and dates in DBNum1 to DBNum3 as Excel does
 in ja-jp
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

[DBNum1] to [DBNum3] write a number in Japanese numerals. ECMA-376 does
not define them for SpreadsheetML (the DBNUM switches of 17.16.4.3 are
Word's), so they follow what Excel for Mac 16.113 shows in the ja-jp
locale. Only DBNum1 was done, only when the format named a locale with
[$-411], and always digit by digit, so [DBNum1]General showed 1234 as
一二三四 where Excel shows 千二百三十四, and DBNum2 and DBNum3 did nothing.

In ja-jp (the format's [$-411], or the workbook's locale):
- With General, the number is written by place value: 千二百三十四,
  一万二千三百四十五, 一億, 一兆. DBNum2 writes daiji with a one before 拾,
  百 and 阡 (壱阡弐百参拾四, 壱萬), and DBNum3 full-width digits with the
  place characters (千２百３十４). Fifteen significant digits are shown,
  the decimals digit by digit (千二百三十四.五), and the digits above 兆
  one by one (一〇〇〇〇兆).
- With other number formats, each digit is replaced (一,二三四), and the
  rounding and separators are those of the format. A section without
  DBNum is not changed, as in Excel ([DBNum1]0;"△"0 shows △1234).
- In dates and times, yyyy and a field that starts with a zero are
  written digit by digit (二〇二六, 〇五) and the other fields by place
  value (令和八年十月八日, 二十三時); DBNum3 makes the digits full-width.

Outside ja-jp nothing changes.

Assisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-5-5
